1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,490
Eight girls at an Illinois high school, and every friendship between them.

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,880
On average, each girl has two and a half friends.

3
00:00:08,066 --> 00:00:10,706
How many friends does the average friend have?

4
00:00:10,966 --> 00:00:11,826
Pick one.

5
00:00:13,933 --> 00:00:21,613
It matters to health researchers choosing whom to watch for flu: random students, or the friends those students name.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,860
The natural guess is two and a half again: every friend is one of these same eight girls.

7
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:32,200
Check that on a rewired class where every girl has two friends.

8
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:35,840
Send a token both ways along every friendship.

9
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,310
Each token reads the friend count where it lands, here always two.

10
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:43,250
Both averages are two: the guess holds.

11
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:47,783
Now restore the real friendships and send the tokens again.

12
00:00:48,043 --> 00:00:50,513
They read one, two, three, or four.

13
00:00:50,773 --> 00:00:54,663
Twenty tokens add up to sixty: friends average three.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,010
The guess was half a friend too low.

15
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:02,560
The tokens piled up unevenly: four on Sue, four on Alice, one on Betty.

16
00:01:03,266 --> 00:01:09,956
Line the girls up by friend count: two at each count, from one to four, so they balance at two and a half.

17
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:15,300
The tokens stack higher on the right, one per friend, so they balance at three.

18
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:19,243
Sue holds four tokens, each worth four: sixteen.

19
00:01:19,503 --> 00:01:24,023
A girl with k friends adds k squared on top, and k on the bottom.

20
00:01:24,466 --> 00:01:33,066
Divide top and bottom by the eight girls: the average of k squared, seven and a half, over the average count, two and a half.

21
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:41,053
Measure each girl's distance from the balance point and square it: two and a quarter at the ends, a quarter in the middle.

22
00:01:41,313 --> 00:01:45,203
They average one and a quarter, the variance, sigma squared.

23
00:01:46,166 --> 00:01:52,406
Split Sue's count, four, into the balance point, two and a half, plus her distance, one and a half.

24
00:01:52,666 --> 00:01:57,356
Squaring gives two and a half squared, her distance squared, and a cross term.

25
00:01:57,956 --> 00:02:06,916
Every girl splits this way, and because the beam balances, the left and right distances add to zero and the cross terms cancel.

26
00:02:07,866 --> 00:02:15,806
So the average of k squared is mu squared plus sigma squared, and friends average mu, plus sigma squared over mu.

27
00:02:16,066 --> 00:02:21,306
Here that's two and a half, plus a half: exactly the extra half friend we measured.

28
00:02:21,566 --> 00:02:24,216
The ring had no spread, so it had no gap.

29
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:31,490
In twenty eleven, Facebook users averaged about a hundred and ninety friends.

30
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:35,950
If every friendship sent tokens, what would the tokens average?

31
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,250
About six hundred and thirty five.

32
00:02:40,510 --> 00:02:46,540
A few users had thousands of friends, so the spread term is more than twice the mean itself.

33
00:02:47,233 --> 00:02:51,683
In twenty oh nine, Christakis and Fowler put this to work at Harvard.

34
00:02:51,943 --> 00:02:58,193
Named friends have more friends, so they sit closer to everyone else, and should catch flu first.

35
00:02:58,453 --> 00:03:06,253
They tracked flu diagnoses in three hundred nineteen random students and four hundred twenty five friends they named.

36
00:03:06,513 --> 00:03:11,993
The friends' curve rose first: fitted curves put them thirteen point nine days ahead.

37
00:03:12,253 --> 00:03:16,063
With self-reported symptoms, the lead shrank to about three.

38
00:03:16,766 --> 00:03:18,316
Back to the eight girls.

39
00:03:18,576 --> 00:03:24,766
Seat by seat, five have fewer friends than their friends average; only Sue and Alice have more.

40
00:03:25,133 --> 00:03:27,753
Betty looks at her one friend and sees four.

41
00:03:28,013 --> 00:03:31,583
Two and a half counts the girls; three counts the friendships.

